WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.540
The Marvel Cinematic Universe grows just a
little bit bigger and usually a whole lot

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.060
weirder with every new movie and TV show that
slides in front of our eyeballs.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.990
When the original Iron Man came out back in
2008, the world he lived in seemed pretty

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.220
grounded in reality.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.660
The strangest thing that could possibly happen
in that film was a rich guy deciding to build

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.460
himself a suit of armor in a cave to become
an international vigilante.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.020
Then fight another rich guy in some other
armor who wanted to be an even worse international

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:28.760
vigilante.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.060
That's pretty strange, but not out of the
realm of possibility, right?

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.801
We're still waiting for Elon Musk and Mark
Zuckerberg to throw down and televise the

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:37.180
whole thing on Facebook Live.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.840
But since then, we've seen gods, monsters,
whole other planets, whole other dimensions,

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.080
and all sorts of magical hijinks unfold in
ways that would make any sane person's head

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:46.580
spin.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.420
So how has the MCU kept feeling as real and
fun as ever?

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.660
Well, former Marvel Studios head of visual
development Charlie Wen has some ideas on

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:55.160
that.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.200
The man in charge of just about all of the
Marvel Cinematic Universe's designs over the

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.221
past decade sat for an exclusive interview
with us to talk about how and why Iron Man

00:01:04.221 --> 00:01:08.560
was actually the perfect superhero to kickstart
the MCU as we know it.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.680
"What was good with Iron Man is that it laid
a foundation for what is tech on Earth.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.400
How do we ground it to Earth and to things
we understand?

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.820
Tech is something easier for us to sort of,
we use it every day, right?"

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.040
He explained that Iron Man's connection with
technology is a convenient starting point

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:32.229
for audiences to find a connection with a
world that looks a lot like our own, but is

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:33.700
also full of super-people.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.619
That helpful foundation revealed that, despite
Iron Man's then-reputation as a lower-tier

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:43.799
comic book character, he was the perfect choice
to kick off Marvel Studios' cinematic ambitions.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.460
"Most people would have thought like, 'Why
are you choosing a character that is sort

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.780
of just kind of a B character in the Marvel
comics world?'

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.460
But you know I think that was kinda a smart
move."

00:01:55.469 --> 00:01:59.409
But technology isn't the only reason Iron
Man was such a strong choice for Marvel's

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:01.980
first film in a shared cinematic universe.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.260
Even though we all use phones and computers
every day, very few moviegoers are also jet-setting

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:07.920
billionaires.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:12.920
That's why Iron Man's alter ego Tony Stark
and the fact that Robert Downey, Jr. played

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.550
him was that other crucial piece of the puzzle
to help audiences connect with the film, as

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:18.760
well as the world it shows on-screen.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:25.420
"It was great getting Iron Man situated and
then having Robert Downey in that role was

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:26.420
amazing.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.230
I mean, he kind of is that character.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.720
it's all the flaws
that he sort of just puts out there, right?"

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:37.740
"How do you make that character and that world
more relatable and less scary?

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:43.700
It's not a Superman character, where it feels
maybe too perfect and too alienating and hard

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.660
for people to resonate with.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.120
It's somebody with lots of flaws.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:53.459
Not all of us have the same kind of flaws
that Iron Man did, or Tony Stark, but I think

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:58.200
it's so obvious that it makes it safe for
anybody to approach."

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.760
Once Iron Man established the baseline of
the MCU's reality, the studio could start

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.140
to explore bigger, weirder ideas.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.450
The next logical step, of course, was to try
to get as weird as possible, as quickly as

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:09.980
possible.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:14.980
Marvel Studios' third movie was 2011's Thor
which happened to be the first film Charlie

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.790
Wen worked on at the company as the head of
design.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:23.361
"When you're trying to write some kind of
story, paint an image, one of the things that

00:03:23.361 --> 00:03:27.170
you do first is you figure out what your limits
are.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.300
The farthest you're gonna go, where are you
gonna start with, and so it's your contrast.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:31.799
What is your contrast?"

00:03:31.799 --> 00:03:36.049
That philosophy determining the contrast was
an approach Wen used while designing all of

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:41.100
the MCU movies he worked on, like Guardians
of the Galaxy characters Gamora and Drax the

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:41.600
Destroyer.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.920
As it turns out, Marvel Studios itself wound
up doing just that by releasing Iron Man and

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.800
Thor so close to each other, since they're
literally worlds apart.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.040
"That was the big thing about laying out Iron
Man and Thor.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.720
It was one of the biggest contrasts that we
could find, and could this still work?

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:03.280
'Cause if this can work, there's a lot of
play in the middle."

